          "evidence": "The top of the May bar is visibly below the 10,000 kWh gridline, while the tops of the Apr, Jan, Jun, Feb, and Mar bars are all at or above the 17,000 kWh level. The May bar is visibly shorter than each of the other five bars.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "3": {
          "evidence": "The vertical axis is labeled 'Grid energy use (kWh)'. The visible tick labels are 10,000, 15,000, 20,000, 25,000, 30,000, 35,000, and 40,000. They increase in value from bottom to top.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "4": {
          "evidence": "At the bottom of the vertical axis, just below the 10,000 tick mark, there is a double-slash symbol (//) indicating a break in the axis scale.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If each bar is bound to its x-axis month label and bar height encodes grid-energy use on the upward y-axis, then May's much shorter bar represents lower use than every other displayed month; if the routing policy treats such an isolated, extreme low value as critical, select critical-anomaly review. Assumes the truncated y-axis still preserves valid height ordering and that an extreme relative low value, without a displayed policy threshold, qualifies as critical.",
        "evidence": "The first part of the reading is supported: the May bar is bound to the 'May' label and its height is lower than all other bars, indicating lower use. However, the second part relies on the condition that 'the routing policy treats such an isolated, extreme low value as critical'. The chart and task context do not provide any routing policy or threshold that defines an 'extreme low value' as critical. The condition is not met by the available evidence. Therefore, the reading as a whole cannot be supported because the necessary policy condition is absent.",
